# TypeScript Rules

## Type Safety
- Use strict TypeScript with proper type definitions
- Define interfaces for all props, API responses, and data structures
- Use type assertions sparingly and prefer type guards
- Export types and interfaces from dedicated files when reused
- Use generic types for reusable components and functions
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use `const assertions` for immutable data

## Type Definitions
```typescript
// Good: Interface for object shapes
interface PokemonCardProps {
  pokemon: Pokemon;
  onSelect?: (pokemon: Pokemon) => void;
}

// Good: Type for unions
type Theme = 'light' | 'dark';

// Good: Generic types
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  data: T;
  status: number;
  message: string;
}
```

## Type Guards
```typescript
// Good: Type guard function
function isPokemon(obj: any): obj is Pokemon {
  return obj && typeof obj.name === 'string' && typeof obj.id === 'number';
}

// Usage
if (isPokemon(data)) {
  // data is now typed as Pokemon
  console.log(data.name);
}
```

## Avoid
- Don't use `any` type
- Don't use `@ts-ignore` without good reason
- Don't use type assertions without proper validation
- Don't create overly complex type definitions